{{- /* Params: Target (bead ID), Mode ("one" = one unit per run, "all" = every unit this run). Decides whether the target is a standalone issue or an epic and which units the same per-issue heuristics apply to. */ -}}
Determine the **units of work** for `{{ .Target }}`:

```bash
bd show {{ .Target }} --children --json     # child beads (is this an epic/parent?)
bd dep tree {{ .Target }}                    # blockers and ordering
```

- **No children (standalone issue)** → the unit is `{{ .Target }}` itself.
- **Has children (epic / parent)** → the epic is a container, not directly implementable.
  The units are its **open children**; treat any child that is itself a parent the same way
  (recurse). Apply the **identical per-issue heuristics** below to **each** child, exactly as
  you would to a standalone issue:
  - skip `closed` children;
  - a child already labelled `implementation-refined` is **already investigated** — do not
    re-investigate it, use its report as the spec;
  - a child that is vague, lacks acceptance criteria, or is blocked is **not ready** — handle
    it per the surrounding step (defer / enrich), never guess;
  - order the remaining children topologically by dependencies.
{{ if eq .Mode "one" }}
  **One unit per run.** Finish before you start: continue a child already `in_progress` with
  well-scoped work; otherwise take the first workable child by the ranking below. Record on the
  epic (`bd comment`) which child you chose and why, so the decision is auditable.
{{ else }}
  **Every unit this run.** Walk all open children in topological order and run the full
  procedure on each; keep a per-child result so the final summary covers the epic as a whole.
{{ end }}
Treat the chosen unit(s) as `<unit>` for the rest of this procedure.
